<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aloha ʻOe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1878 (her manuscript is dated <a href="/years/1878">1878</a>; the ride that inspired it 1877 or 1878)</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Aloha ʻOe&quot; (&quot;Farewell to Thee&quot;) was written about 1878 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>, then a princess of the Hawaiian Kingdom. The accounts of its origin differ in detail, but all agree that it was inspired by a farewell embrace given by Colonel James Harbottle Boyd at the end of a horseback trip that the princess and her party made in 1877 or 1878 to the Boyd ranch at Maunawili, on the windward side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The accounts of the song's beginning differ over who received Colonel James Harbottle Boyd's farewell embrace: some say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>'s sister, Princess Likelike Cleghorn, others a young lady at the ranch. In the most familiar version the parting set the princess humming on the ride home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>. Charles Wilson remarked that the tune sounded like The Lone Rock by the Sea, and she is said to have agreed. When the party rested in an orange grove on the Honolulu side of the Pali the others joined in, and the song was finished later at Washington Place.</p><p>The Hawaiʻi State Archives keep a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in her hand, dated 1878, with the score, the Hawaiian words and her own English translation. A note evidently added later reads that it was composed at Maunawili in 1878, played by the Royal Hawaiian Band in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> in August 1883, and became very popular.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The song is a Hawaiian farewell and is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>'s best-known work. Parts of it resemble The Lone Rock by the Sea and the chorus of George Frederick Root's 1854 song There's Music in the Air. The tune Wilson recognised was The Rock Beside the Sea, published by Charles Crozat Converse in 1857 and itself derived from a Croatian or Serbian folk song, Sedi Mara na kamen studencu. Its chorus was later used as a counterpoint in the jazz song Hula Lou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Berliner Gramophone released the first known recording in 1898, and Columbia Records recorded a duet by Nani Alapai and Henry N. Clark in 1911. A 1913 recording by the Hawaiian Quintette, with Walter Kolomoku on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> guitar, was chosen in 2025 for the National Recording Registry of the Library of Congress. Later singers included Bing Crosby in 1936 and Elvis Presley, for the film Blue Hawaii, in 1961.</p><p>The song became a common cultural symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. It closed the 1932 Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> as the flame was put out, served as a stock cue in Warner Bros. cartoons, and gave its name to a farewell ceremony for graduating seniors of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> from 1926.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aloha ʻOe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1887 Liliʻuokalani’s song “<b>Aloha ʻOe</b>” was already famous in the United States.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Aloha ʻOe</b>&quot; (&quot;Farewell to Thee&quot;) was written about 1878 by Liliʻuokalani, then a princess of the Hawaiian Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a></li></ul></section>
